window.ND=window.ND||{};Object.assign(ND,{"bl|Composition":{"d":"## Blood – overview\nBlood is a fluid connective tissue: formed elements suspended in plasma. Volume ≈ 7–8% of body weight (≈ 5 L in a 70-kg adult; ≈ 75–80 mL/kg in men, slightly less in women; ≈ 85 mL/kg in newborns). Specific gravity of whole blood 1.052–1.061, plasma 1.022–1.026, RBCs ≈ 1.090. Viscosity ≈ 3–4 times water (mainly due to RBCs); plasma ≈ 1.8 times water. pH 7.35–7.45. Temperature ≈ 38 °C.\n\n## Components\n- **Plasma** ≈ 55% of blood volume: water ≈ 91–92%, solids ≈ 8–9% (proteins 6.4–8.3 g/dL; inorganic salts ≈ 0.9%; organic substances – glucose, lipids, urea, creatinine, amino acids, hormones, enzymes). Serum = plasma minus fibrinogen and clotting factors consumed in clotting.\n- **Formed elements** ≈ 45% (packed cell volume / haematocrit): RBCs, WBCs and platelets. The buffy coat (WBCs + platelets) is a thin layer ≈ 1% between plasma and RBCs after centrifugation.\n\n## Normal values (adult, commonly accepted)\n- RBC: 4.5–6.0 million/µL (male), 4.0–5.5 million/µL (female).\n- Haemoglobin: 13–17 g/dL (male), 12–15 g/dL (female); newborn 16–22 g/dL.\n- PCV/Hct: 40–50% (male), 36–45% (female).\n- WBC: 4,000–11,000/µL.\n- Platelets: 1.5–4.0 lakh/µL.\n- ESR (Westergren): 0–15 mm/h (male), 0–20 mm/h (female).\n- Plasma proteins: total 6.4–8.3 g/dL; albumin 3.5–5.0; globulin 2.0–3.5; fibrinogen 0.2–0.4 g/dL; A:G ratio 1.2–2:1.\n\n## Functions of blood\n- **Transport**: respiratory gases (O₂, CO₂), nutrients, wastes (urea, creatinine, bilirubin), hormones, heat, drugs.\n- **Homeostasis**: buffering (Hb, plasma proteins, HCO₃⁻), water and electrolyte balance, temperature distribution.\n- **Defence**: leucocytes, antibodies, complement.\n- **Haemostasis**: platelets and clotting factors.\n- **Oncotic pressure** by plasma proteins maintains blood volume.\n- Storage of reserve proteins; transport of iron (transferrin) and other substances.\n\n## Haematocrit and viscosity\nHaematocrit determines viscosity: at Hct 40% viscosity ≈ 3 × water; at 60–70% (polycythaemia) it can exceed 10 × water, increasing peripheral resistance and cardiac work. Viscosity is lower in small vessels (Fåhraeus–Lindqvist effect – axial streaming of RBCs).\n\n## Determination of blood volume\nPlasma volume by Evans blue or ¹²⁵I-albumin; red cell volume by ⁵¹Cr-labelled RBCs; blood volume = plasma volume × 100/(100 − Hct).\n\n## Haematopoiesis sites\n- Yolk sac (mesoblastic, from 3rd week) → liver (main from ~6 weeks to mid-gestation) and spleen → bone marrow (from ~5th month; sole site after birth).\n- In children all bones are active; in adults red marrow is limited to the flat bones (vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, skull) and proximal ends of femur and humerus. Extramedullary haematopoiesis may restart in liver and spleen in disease (myelofibrosis, thalassaemia).\n- All cells arise from pluripotent haematopoietic stem cells (CD34⁺) → myeloid and lymphoid progenitors, under cytokines (erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, G-CSF, GM-CSF, interleukins).\n\n## Applied\n- Anaemia (↓Hb), polycythaemia (↑RBC/Hct), leucocytosis/leucopenia, thrombocytopenia.\n- Haemoconcentration in dehydration and burns (↑Hct); haemodilution in pregnancy (physiological anaemia).\n- Bone marrow aspiration from posterior iliac crest (adults) or tibia (infants).","q":{"b":["Blood volume ≈ 7–8% of body weight (≈ 5 L).","Plasma ≈ 55%, cells ≈ 45%; buffy coat = WBC + platelets.","Serum = plasma minus fibrinogen (and consumed clotting factors).","Plasma: 91–92% water, ~7 g/dL protein.","Viscosity of blood ≈ 3–4× water, determined mainly by Hct.","Functions: transport, defence, haemostasis, buffering, temperature, oncotic pressure.","Haematopoiesis: yolk sac → liver/spleen → bone marrow.","Adult red marrow: flat bones and proximal femur/humerus.","Blood volume = plasma volume × 100/(100 − Hct).","Haemoconcentration in dehydration/burns; haemodilution in pregnancy."],"n":["Hb: M 13–17, F 12–15 g/dL","RBC: M 4.5–6.0, F 4.0–5.5 million/µL","WBC 4,000–11,000/µL; platelets 1.5–4 lakh/µL","PCV: M 40–50%, F 36–45%","Specific gravity blood 1.052–1.061"],"m":"Haematopoiesis sites **\"Young Liver Synthesises Blood\"** – Yolk sac, Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow.","v":["Difference between plasma and serum? – serum lacks fibrinogen and consumed clotting factors.","Main determinant of blood viscosity? – haematocrit (RBC concentration).","Chief site of haematopoiesis in the mid-gestation fetus? – liver."]}},"bl|RBC":{"d":"## Structure\nMature red blood cells are biconcave, non-nucleated discs, diameter ≈ 7.2–7.8 µm (mean 7.5), thickness ≈ 2.5 µm at the rim and ≈ 1 µm at the centre, volume (MCV) 80–96 fL. They lack nucleus, mitochondria and ribosomes, so they cannot synthesise protein or divide and depend on glycolysis for energy.\n\n**Advantages of the biconcave shape**: large surface area relative to volume for gas exchange; short diffusion distance; the cell can swell a lot without rupturing (spare membrane); great deformability to pass capillaries as narrow as 3 µm. Shape is maintained by the cytoskeleton (spectrin, actin, ankyrin, band 4.1, band 3).\n\n## Composition\n≈ 60–65% water, ≈ 33% haemoglobin (≈ 34 g/dL of cells), enzymes (carbonic anhydrase, glycolytic enzymes, G6PD), high K⁺, membrane lipids.\n\n## Metabolism\n- **Embden–Meyerhof (glycolytic) pathway** – ATP (runs Na⁺–K⁺ pump, maintains shape) and NADH (used by methaemoglobin reductase to keep iron ferrous).\n- **Rapoport–Luebering shunt** – forms **2,3-BPG**, which lowers Hb's O₂ affinity (right shift).\n- **Hexose monophosphate shunt** – NADPH via G6PD keeps glutathione reduced, protecting Hb and membrane from oxidants.\n\n## Functions\n- O₂ transport (bound to Hb) and CO₂ transport (bicarbonate formed via carbonic anhydrase; carbamino-Hb).\n- Buffering – Hb is the main non-bicarbonate blood buffer.\n- Chloride shift through band 3 (Cl⁻–HCO₃⁻ exchanger).\n- Determines blood viscosity; carries blood-group antigens.\n\n## Normal values\n- Count: 4.5–6.0 million/µL (male), 4.0–5.5 (female); newborn 6–7 million.\n- Red cell indices: MCV = PCV × 10 / RBC (millions) = 80–96 fL; MCH = Hb × 10 / RBC = 27–32 pg; MCHC = Hb × 100 / PCV = 32–36 g/dL; RDW 11.5–14.5%.\n- Reticulocytes 0.5–2.5% (2–6% in newborn).\n\n## Variations in RBC count\n- **Physiological increase**: newborn, high altitude, muscular exercise, emotion, males, hot climate (haemoconcentration).\n- **Physiological decrease**: pregnancy (haemodilution), females, after sleep, old age.\n- **Pathological increase (polycythaemia)**: primary – polycythaemia vera; secondary – chronic hypoxia (COPD, cyanotic heart disease, altitude), EPO-secreting tumours; relative – dehydration, burns.\n- **Pathological decrease**: anaemias.\n\n## Lifespan and destruction\nAverage lifespan ≈ 120 days. Ageing cells lose enzymes, become rigid and are trapped and phagocytosed in the spleen (the \"graveyard of RBCs\"), liver and marrow. Hb → globin (amino acids) + haem → iron (recycled) + biliverdin → bilirubin. About 6–7 g Hb is broken down each day.\n\n## Abnormal sizes and shapes\n- **Anisocytosis**: microcytes (< 6 µm – iron deficiency, thalassaemia), macrocytes (> 9 µm – B12/folate deficiency).\n- **Poikilocytosis**: spherocytes (hereditary spherocytosis), sickle cells (HbS), target cells (thalassaemia, liver disease), elliptocytes, schistocytes (microangiopathy), acanthocytes, tear-drop cells (myelofibrosis).\n- **Rouleaux** – stacking of RBCs, increased by fibrinogen and globulins (↑ESR).\n\n## Applied\n- G6PD deficiency – haemolysis with primaquine, sulphonamides, fava beans; Heinz bodies, bite cells.\n- Hereditary spherocytosis – increased osmotic fragility, splenomegaly; splenectomy helps.\n- Sickle-cell disease – HbS polymerises when deoxygenated → vaso-occlusive crises.","q":{"b":["Biconcave, non-nucleated; diameter ≈ 7.5 µm, 2.5 µm thick at edge, 1 µm at centre.","No mitochondria – ATP only from glycolysis.","Biconcavity: ↑surface area, deformability, can swell without bursting.","Rapoport–Luebering shunt → 2,3-BPG (↓O₂ affinity).","HMP shunt/G6PD → NADPH → reduced glutathione (antioxidant).","Indices: MCV 80–96 fL, MCH 27–32 pg, MCHC 32–36 g/dL.","Lifespan 120 days; destroyed mainly in spleen.","Physiological polycythaemia: newborn, high altitude.","Microcytes – iron deficiency/thalassaemia; macrocytes – B12/folate deficiency.","Spherocytes ↑ osmotic fragility; target cells – thalassaemia, liver disease.","Band 3 = Cl⁻–HCO₃⁻ exchanger (chloride shift)."],"n":["RBC: M 4.5–6.0; F 4.0–5.5 million/µL","MCV 80–96 fL; MCH 27–32 pg; MCHC 32–36 g/dL","Reticulocytes 0.5–2.5%","Lifespan 120 days","Diameter 7.5 µm"],"m":"Indices: **MCV = PCV/RBC, MCH = Hb/RBC, MCHC = Hb/PCV** – \"Volume, Haemoglobin, Concentration\".","v":["Why is the RBC biconcave? – more surface area and deformability.","Normal MCHC? – 32–36 g/dL.","Graveyard of RBCs? – spleen."]}},"bl|Erythropoiesis":{"d":"## Definition and sites\nErythropoiesis is the formation of red cells. Sites: yolk sac (first weeks) → liver and spleen (2nd trimester) → red bone marrow (from 5th month; the only site after birth). In adults: vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, skull, proximal femur and humerus. About 2–2.5 million RBCs are produced every second.\n\n## Stages\nPluripotent stem cell → common myeloid progenitor → BFU-E → CFU-E →\n1. **Proerythroblast** – large (15–20 µm), nucleus with nucleoli, deeply basophilic cytoplasm.\n2. **Early (basophilic) normoblast** – nucleoli disappear, intense basophilia (ribosomes).\n3. **Intermediate (polychromatophilic) normoblast** – Hb first appears (mixed blue–pink); last stage capable of mitosis.\n4. **Late (orthochromatic) normoblast** – small pyknotic nucleus, acidophilic cytoplasm; nucleus extruded.\n5. **Reticulocyte** – no nucleus, residual RNA network seen with supravital stains (new methylene blue, brilliant cresyl blue); 1–2 days in marrow, ~1 day in blood.\n6. **Erythrocyte**.\nProerythroblast to RBC takes ≈ 7 days. During maturation: cell size falls, nucleus condenses and is lost, nucleoli disappear, Hb increases, cytoplasm turns from basophilic to acidophilic.\n\n## Regulation – erythropoietin (EPO)\n- Glycoprotein (≈ 34 kDa); ≈ 85–90% from peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex, 10–15% from liver (main fetal source).\n- **Stimulus: tissue hypoxia** – sensed through hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF-1α), stabilised when O₂ is low. Causes: anaemia, haemorrhage, high altitude, lung disease, heart failure.\n- Acts via JAK2-STAT receptors on CFU-E and proerythroblasts → proliferation, differentiation, less apoptosis, faster Hb synthesis. New RBCs appear after ≈ 5 days.\n- Other stimulators: androgens (why men have higher Hb), thyroxine, cortisol, growth hormone. Oestrogens mildly inhibit. Renal failure → EPO deficiency.\n\n## Nutritional requirements\n- **Proteins** – for globin.\n- **Iron** – for haem; ≈ 20–25 mg/day used, mostly recycled.\n- **Vitamin B12 and folic acid** – needed for DNA synthesis. Deficiency → nuclear maturation lags behind cytoplasm → megaloblasts → macrocytic anaemia. B12 absorption needs intrinsic factor (parietal cells) and occurs in terminal ileum; stores 2–5 mg last 3–6 years; folate stores last ≈ 3–4 months.\n- **Vitamin B6** – cofactor for ALA synthase (deficiency → sideroblastic anaemia).\n- Vitamin C, copper, cobalt (in B12), vitamin E.\n\n## Assessment\nReticulocyte count reflects marrow output (0.5–2.5%). Reticulocytosis follows haemorrhage, haemolysis and treatment of nutritional anaemia (peaks 5–10 days after starting iron or B12).\n\n## Applied\n- Anaemia of chronic kidney disease – EPO deficiency; recombinant EPO (misused in sport as blood doping).\n- Secondary polycythaemia – high altitude, COPD, cyanotic heart disease, EPO-secreting renal tumours.\n- Polycythaemia vera – JAK2 V617F mutation, EPO low.\n- Pernicious anaemia – autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor.","q":{"b":["Sites: yolk sac → liver/spleen → bone marrow (after birth only marrow).","Proerythroblast → early → intermediate → late normoblast → reticulocyte → RBC.","Hb first appears in the intermediate (polychromatophilic) normoblast.","Nucleus extruded at late normoblast; last mitosis at intermediate stage.","Maturation ≈ 7 days; reticulocyte ~1 day in blood.","EPO: 85–90% renal peritubular cells; stimulus = hypoxia via HIF-1α.","EPO acts on CFU-E via JAK2-STAT; RBCs rise after ≈ 5 days.","Androgens stimulate, oestrogens mildly inhibit.","B12 & folate needed for DNA synthesis → megaloblastic anaemia when deficient.","B12 needs intrinsic factor; absorbed in terminal ileum.","Reticulocyte count = index of marrow activity."],"n":["Reticulocytes 0.5–2.5%","≈ 2.5 million RBC/s","EPO ≈ 34 kDa","Maturation ≈ 7 days","B12 stores 3–6 yr; folate 3–4 months"],"m":"Stages **\"Please Eat In Lovely Restaurants Regularly\"** – Pro, Early, Intermediate, Late, Reticulocyte, RBC.","v":["Main site of EPO production? – peritubular interstitial cells of renal cortex.","At which stage does Hb first appear? – intermediate normoblast.","Stain for reticulocytes? – supravital (new methylene blue/brilliant cresyl blue)."]}},"bl|Iron & anaemia":{"d":"## Body iron\nTotal ≈ 3–4 g (≈ 50 mg/kg men, 35–40 mg/kg women): haemoglobin ≈ 65–70%; stores (ferritin, haemosiderin in liver, spleen, marrow) ≈ 15–30%; myoglobin ≈ 4%; enzymes ≈ 1%; transferrin-bound ≈ 0.1%.\n\n## Requirement and loss\nLoss ≈ 1 mg/day in men (shed cells, sweat, bile); menstruating women lose ≈ 0.5–1 mg/day more. Pregnancy needs ≈ 1000 mg extra. Diet has 10–20 mg/day; only 5–10% (1–2 mg) is absorbed, rising 2–4-fold in deficiency.\n\n## Absorption (duodenum, upper jejunum)\n- **Haem iron** (meat) – well absorbed; iron released inside the enterocyte by haem oxygenase.\n- **Non-haem iron** – Fe³⁺ reduced to Fe²⁺ by duodenal cytochrome b and vitamin C; enters via **DMT-1**.\n- In the cell: stored as ferritin (lost when cell sheds) or exported by **ferroportin**, oxidised by **hephaestin**, bound to **transferrin**.\n- **Hepcidin** (liver) degrades ferroportin → ↓absorption and ↓macrophage release. ↑ in iron overload and inflammation; ↓ in deficiency, hypoxia, active erythropoiesis.\n- Enhancers: vitamin C, HCl, Fe²⁺ form, amino acids. Inhibitors: phytates, oxalates, phosphates, tannins (tea), calcium, antacids, achlorhydria.\n\n## Transport and storage\nTransferrin (β-globulin) carries 2 Fe³⁺, normally ≈ 30% saturated. Serum iron 60–170 µg/dL; TIBC 250–400 µg/dL. Ferritin – soluble store; serum ferritin 15–300 ng/mL (< 15 = deficiency). Haemosiderin – insoluble aggregate.\n\n## Anaemia\nDefinition (WHO): Hb < 13 g/dL men, < 12 non-pregnant women, < 11 in pregnancy and children 6–59 months.\n**Morphological classification**:\n- Microcytic hypochromic – iron deficiency, thalassaemia, sideroblastic anaemia, lead poisoning, some chronic disease.\n- Normocytic normochromic – acute blood loss, haemolysis, aplastic anaemia, CKD, chronic disease.\n- Macrocytic – megaloblastic (B12, folate), alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism.\n**Aetiological**: blood loss; impaired production (nutritional, marrow failure, infiltration); increased destruction (hereditary – spherocytosis, G6PD, sickle cell, thalassaemia; acquired – autoimmune, malaria, drugs, mismatched transfusion).\n\n## Iron-deficiency anaemia (commonest)\nCauses: poor intake, increased demand (pregnancy, growth), chronic blood loss (hookworm, menorrhagia, GI bleeding, piles), malabsorption.\nStages: store depletion (↓ferritin) → iron-deficient erythropoiesis (↓serum iron, ↑TIBC, ↓saturation) → microcytic hypochromic anaemia.\nFeatures: fatigue, pallor, breathlessness, koilonychia, angular stomatitis, glossitis, pica, Plummer–Vinson syndrome.\nLab: ↓Hb, MCV, MCH, MCHC; ↑RDW; saturation < 16%; pencil cells.\n\n## Physiological effects of anaemia\n↓O₂ content → hypoxia → compensation: ↑cardiac output (tachycardia, flow murmurs), ↓viscosity and peripheral resistance, ↑2,3-BPG, ↑EPO. Severe anaemia → high-output heart failure.\n\n## Applied\n- Thalassaemia trait: microcytosis with normal ferritin, ↑HbA₂; Mentzer index (MCV/RBC) < 13.\n- Anaemia of chronic disease – high hepcidin traps iron (↓serum iron, ↓TIBC, ↑ferritin).\n- Haemochromatosis – iron overload (low hepcidin).\n- Anaemia Mukt Bharat – IFA supplementation in India.","q":{"b":["Body iron 3–4 g; ~2/3 in Hb.","Daily loss ≈ 1 mg (men); more in menstruating women.","Duodenum: Fe²⁺ via DMT-1; exported by ferroportin; hephaestin oxidises.","Hepcidin blocks ferroportin – master regulator (↑ in inflammation).","Vitamin C enhances; phytates, tannins, calcium inhibit.","Ferritin reflects stores (< 15 ng/mL = deficiency).","WHO anaemia: Hb < 13 men, < 12 women, < 11 pregnancy.","Microcytic: iron deficiency, thalassaemia, sideroblastic, lead.","Macrocytic: B12/folate deficiency, alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism.","IDA: ↓ferritin, ↓serum iron, ↑TIBC, ↓saturation.","Compensation: ↑CO, ↑2,3-BPG, ↑EPO, ↓viscosity."],"n":["Serum iron 60–170 µg/dL; TIBC 250–400","Saturation 20–45%","Ferritin 15–300 ng/mL","Absorption 1–2 mg/day","Pregnancy ≈ 1000 mg extra"],"m":"Microcytic causes **\"TAILS\"** – Thalassaemia, Anaemia of chronic disease, Iron deficiency, Lead, Sideroblastic.","v":["Site of iron absorption? – duodenum.","Best test for iron stores? – serum ferritin.","Hormone controlling iron absorption? – hepcidin."]}},"bl|Jaundice":{"d":"## Definition\nJaundice is yellow colour of skin, sclera and mucosa due to raised bilirubin. Normal serum bilirubin 0.2–1.2 mg/dL (conjugated < 0.3). Clinically visible above ≈ 2–3 mg/dL (sclera first); 1.2–2 mg/dL is latent jaundice.\n\n## Bilirubin metabolism\n1. **Formation** – ≈ 80% from Hb of old RBCs in macrophages; ≈ 20% from ineffective erythropoiesis and other haem proteins. ≈ 250–350 mg/day. Haem → (haem oxygenase) → biliverdin + Fe + CO → (biliverdin reductase) → unconjugated bilirubin.\n2. **Transport** – unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is lipid-soluble, carried on **albumin**, not filtered (absent from urine). Displaced by sulphonamides, salicylates, free fatty acids.\n3. **Uptake** by hepatocytes (OATP) and binding to ligandin.\n4. **Conjugation** – **UDP-glucuronyl transferase (UGT1A1)** → bilirubin glucuronides (direct, water-soluble).\n5. **Excretion** into bile via **MRP2** – the rate-limiting step.\n6. **Intestine** – bacteria form **urobilinogen**; most becomes stercobilin (brown faeces); 10–20% reabsorbed (enterohepatic), small amount in urine as urobilin (1–4 mg/day).\n\n## Van den Bergh reaction\nConjugated bilirubin reacts directly with diazo reagent; unconjugated only after alcohol (indirect); biphasic in hepatic jaundice.\n\n## Types\n**Pre-hepatic (haemolytic)** – haemolytic anaemias, malaria, mismatched transfusion: ↑unconjugated; no bilirubin in urine (**acholuric**); ↑urine urobilinogen; dark stools; normal liver enzymes; anaemia, reticulocytosis.\n**Hepatic (hepatocellular)** – viral hepatitis, alcohol, drugs, cirrhosis: both fractions ↑; bilirubinuria; ALT/AST markedly ↑.\n**Post-hepatic (obstructive)** – CBD stones, carcinoma head of pancreas, strictures: ↑conjugated; dark urine with bilirubin and bile salts (Hay's test); urobilinogen absent; **clay-coloured stools**, steatorrhoea; **pruritus**; bradycardia; ALP and GGT markedly ↑; vitamin K malabsorption → ↑PT correctable by parenteral vitamin K.\n\n## Inherited\nGilbert (mild ↓UGT, benign, worse on fasting); Crigler–Najjar I (absent UGT, kernicterus) and II; Dubin–Johnson (MRP2 defect, black liver) and Rotor (conjugated).\n\n## Neonatal jaundice\nPhysiological: appears after 24 h, peaks day 3–5, clears by 2 weeks. Causes: high RBC mass, shorter fetal RBC life (80–90 days), immature UGT, ↑enterohepatic circulation. Pathological: within 24 h, rapid rise, > 15 mg/dL, conjugated, or prolonged. **Kernicterus** – unconjugated bilirubin enters immature brain (basal ganglia). Treatment: phototherapy (blue light 450–460 nm forms water-soluble lumirubin), exchange transfusion.","q":{"b":["Bilirubin 0.2–1.2 mg/dL; visible > 2–3 mg/dL (sclera first).","Haem → biliverdin → unconjugated bilirubin (albumin-bound, not in urine).","Conjugation by UDP-glucuronyl transferase.","Excretion via MRP2 = rate-limiting step.","Gut bacteria → urobilinogen → stercobilin / urobilin.","Haemolytic: unconjugated ↑, acholuric, ↑urine urobilinogen.","Obstructive: conjugated ↑, bilirubin + bile salts in urine, clay stools, pruritus, ↑ALP.","Hepatocellular: both ↑, ALT/AST ↑↑.","Van den Bergh: direct = conjugated.","Physiological neonatal jaundice after 24 h, peaks day 3–5.","Kernicterus = unconjugated bilirubin in basal ganglia; phototherapy 450–460 nm.","Gilbert benign unconjugated; Dubin–Johnson conjugated."],"n":["Bilirubin 0.2–1.2 mg/dL","Production 250–350 mg/day","Urine urobilinogen 1–4 mg/day","Phototherapy 450–460 nm"],"m":"Obstructive jaundice: **\"Dark urine, Pale stool, Itchy skin\"**; haemolytic = **acholuric**.","v":["Why no bilirubin in urine in haemolytic jaundice? – unconjugated bilirubin is albumin-bound and insoluble.","Enzyme conjugating bilirubin? – UDP-glucuronyl transferase.","Cause of clay-coloured stools? – no stercobilin due to biliary obstruction."]}},"bl|WBC":{"d":"## General\nLeucocytes are nucleated defence cells. Total count 4,000–11,000/µL (newborn 10,000–25,000). They act mainly in tissues and show **amoeboid movement**, **diapedesis** through capillary walls, **chemotaxis** and **phagocytosis**.\n\n## Classification and differential count (adult)\n**Granulocytes**\n- **Neutrophils** 40–75%: 10–14 µm, 2–5-lobed nucleus, fine pink-violet granules; drumstick (Barr body) in females.\n- **Eosinophils** 1–6%: bilobed nucleus, coarse orange-red granules (major basic protein, cationic protein, peroxidase).\n- **Basophils** 0–1%: S-shaped nucleus hidden by dark-blue granules (histamine, heparin).\n**Agranulocytes**\n- **Lymphocytes** 20–45%: small (7–9 µm) or large; round nucleus, thin rim of cytoplasm.\n- **Monocytes** 2–10%: largest (14–20 µm), kidney-shaped nucleus; become tissue macrophages.\n\n## Lifespan\nNeutrophils 4–8 h in blood, 4–5 days in tissue; monocytes 10–20 h in blood, months–years as macrophages; lymphocytes weeks to years.\n\n## Functions\n**Neutrophils** – first cellular defence: margination (selectins) → adhesion (integrins–ICAM) → diapedesis → chemotaxis (C5a, LTB₄, IL-8, bacterial peptides) → phagocytosis aided by opsonins (IgG, C3b). Killing: **oxygen-dependent** respiratory burst (NADPH oxidase → superoxide → H₂O₂; myeloperoxidase → HOCl) and **oxygen-independent** (lysozyme, defensins, lactoferrin). Pus = dead neutrophils and debris.\n**Eosinophils** – kill helminths (major basic protein); dampen allergy (histaminase, arylsulphatase); increased in allergy.\n**Basophils** – like mast cells; IgE receptors; histamine, heparin, leukotrienes in type I hypersensitivity.\n**Monocyte–macrophage (reticuloendothelial) system** – Kupffer cells, alveolar macrophages, microglia, osteoclasts, Langerhans cells: phagocytosis, antigen presentation (MHC II), cytokines (IL-1, TNF-α), iron recycling.\n**Lymphocytes** – B cells (antibodies), T cells (CD4 helper, CD8 cytotoxic, regulatory), NK cells.\n\n## Leucopoiesis\nMyeloblast → promyelocyte → myelocyte → metamyelocyte → band → segmented neutrophil; regulated by G-CSF, GM-CSF, M-CSF, IL-3, IL-5. A marginated pool equals the circulating pool (adrenaline and exercise demarginate).\n\n## Variations\n- **Leucocytosis**: physiological – newborn, exercise, pregnancy, emotion; pathological – bacterial infection (neutrophilia), leukaemia, MI, steroids.\n- **Leucopenia**: typhoid, viral infections (dengue), drugs, aplastic anaemia, radiation, hypersplenism.\n- **Eosinophilia** – allergy, parasites, Hodgkin lymphoma; **eosinopenia** – steroids.\n- **Lymphocytosis** – viral infection, TB, whooping cough, CLL.\n- **Monocytosis** – TB, malaria, kala-azar, endocarditis. **Basophilia** – CML.\n- **Shift to left** – band forms in acute infection; hypersegmentation (right shift) in megaloblastic anaemia.\n\n## Applied\nChronic granulomatous disease (NADPH oxidase defect); leucocyte adhesion deficiency (CD18); Chediak–Higashi syndrome.","q":{"b":["TLC 4,000–11,000/µL; newborn up to 25,000.","DLC: N 40–75, L 20–45, M 2–10, E 1–6, B 0–1%.","Neutrophil steps: margination, adhesion, diapedesis, chemotaxis, phagocytosis.","Respiratory burst: NADPH oxidase → superoxide → H₂O₂ → HOCl.","Eosinophils: parasites and allergy.","Basophils: histamine, heparin; IgE receptors.","Monocytes → macrophages (RES).","Lymphocytes: B, T and NK cells.","Neutrophilia – bacterial; lymphocytosis – viral, TB, pertussis.","Leucopenia – typhoid, dengue, drugs.","Left shift = band forms; hypersegmentation = B12/folate deficiency.","CGD = NADPH oxidase defect."],"n":["TLC 4,000–11,000/µL","Neutrophils 40–75%; lymphocytes 20–45%","Neutrophil blood life 4–8 h","Monocyte 14–20 µm"],"m":"**\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\"** – N > L > M > E > B.","v":["Most numerous WBC? – neutrophil.","Largest WBC? – monocyte.","Defect in chronic granulomatous disease? – NADPH oxidase."]}},"bl|Immunoglobulins":{"d":"## Definition\nImmunoglobulins are glycoprotein antibodies (γ-globulins) secreted by **plasma cells** (from B lymphocytes); ≈ 20% of plasma proteins.\n\n## Structure\nY-shaped monomer: two identical **heavy chains** (≈ 50 kDa) and two **light chains** (≈ 25 kDa, κ or λ), joined by disulphide bonds.\n- Variable (V) regions at the N-terminal end; hypervariable CDRs form the antigen-binding site.\n- Papain → 2 **Fab** (antigen binding) + 1 **Fc** (binds complement, Fc receptors, crosses placenta). Pepsin → F(ab')₂.\n- Hinge region gives flexibility.\n- Heavy-chain class decides isotype: γ, α, µ, δ, ε.\n- J chain links IgM pentamer and IgA dimer; secretory component protects secretory IgA.\n\n## Classes\n**IgG** – 75–80% (800–1600 mg/dL); monomer; t½ ≈ 21–23 days; only Ig to **cross the placenta** (FcRn); main antibody of secondary response; opsonises, activates complement, neutralises toxins, ADCC; anti-D is IgG.\n**IgA** – 10–15%; secretory dimer in saliva, tears, colostrum, gut and airway mucus; mucosal defence; most produced Ig overall.\n**IgM** – 5–10%; **pentamer**, largest (≈ 900 kDa); first in primary response and first made by fetus (cord IgM = intrauterine infection); best at complement activation and agglutination; natural anti-A/anti-B; monomer is B-cell receptor; does not cross placenta.\n**IgD** – < 1%; B-cell surface receptor.\n**IgE** – trace; binds mast cells and basophils → type I hypersensitivity; anti-helminth.\n\n## Primary vs secondary response\n- Primary: lag 7–10 days, low titre, mainly IgM, short-lived, low affinity.\n- Secondary: lag 1–3 days, high titre, mainly IgG, long-lasting, high affinity (memory B cells; basis of boosters).\n\n## Functions\nNeutralisation; agglutination and precipitation; opsonisation; complement activation (classical pathway – IgM > IgG3 > IgG1) → lysis; ADCC; mast-cell sensitisation (IgE); mucosal protection (IgA).\n\n## Diversity\nVDJ recombination (RAG), junctional diversity, heavy–light pairing, somatic hypermutation; class switching requires CD40L of helper T cells and cytokines (IL-4 → IgE).\n\n## Applied\nMultiple myeloma – M-band, Bence Jones proteins (light chains); Bruton agammaglobulinaemia (BTK); selective IgA deficiency (commonest; anaphylaxis with transfusion); hyper-IgM syndrome (CD40L); monoclonal antibodies in therapy.","q":{"b":["Made by plasma cells; ≈ 20% of plasma proteins.","2 heavy + 2 light (κ/λ) chains; Fab binds antigen, Fc for effector functions.","IgG: most abundant, crosses placenta, secondary response, t½ ≈ 21 days.","IgA: secretions, colostrum, mucosal immunity (dimer).","IgM: pentamer, largest, first in primary response, best complement activator.","IgE: mast cells, type I hypersensitivity, helminths.","IgD: B-cell receptor.","Primary: IgM, lag 7–10 days; secondary: IgG, faster, higher titre.","Cord-blood IgM = intrauterine infection.","Functions: neutralise, agglutinate, opsonise, complement, ADCC.","Selective IgA deficiency is the commonest primary immunodeficiency."],"n":["IgG 75–80%; IgA 10–15%; IgM 5–10%","IgG t½ ≈ 21–23 days","IgM ≈ 900 kDa","Primary lag 7–10 days"],"m":"**\"GAMED\"** = order of concentration; Ig**G** **G**oes across placenta; Ig**M** = **M**acro, first.","v":["Which Ig crosses the placenta? – IgG.","Which Ig is a pentamer? – IgM.","Ig in colostrum? – secretory IgA."]}},"bl|Platelets":{"d":"## Structure\nPlatelets are 2–4 µm, disc-shaped, non-nucleated fragments of bone-marrow **megakaryocytes** (each yields ≈ 1000–5000). Count 1.5–4.0 lakh/µL; lifespan 8–12 days; about one-third pooled in the spleen; removed by spleen and liver.\n- Membrane glycoproteins: **GP Ib-IX-V** (binds vWF), **GP IIb/IIIa** (binds fibrinogen), GP Ia/IIa and GP VI (collagen); phospholipid surface (platelet factor 3).\n- Dense tubular system (Ca²⁺ store, prostaglandin synthesis); open canalicular system.\n- Contractile **thrombosthenin** (actin–myosin) for clot retraction; microtubules keep disc shape.\n- **α-granules**: fibrinogen, vWF, factor V, PF4, PDGF, P-selectin. **Dense granules**: ADP, ATP, Ca²⁺, serotonin.\n\n## Thrombopoiesis\nStem cell → megakaryoblast → megakaryocyte (polyploid up to 64N by endomitosis) → cytoplasmic fragmentation. Regulated by **thrombopoietin** (mainly liver); TPO level falls as platelet mass rises. IL-6 and IL-11 also stimulate.\n\n## Functions\n1. **Platelet plug** – **adhesion** to exposed collagen via vWF–GP Ib; **activation** (shape change, pseudopods) and **release** of ADP, serotonin, TXA₂; **aggregation** – ADP and TXA₂ (via COX-1) recruit more platelets, GP IIb/IIIa links them by fibrinogen.\n2. **Vasoconstriction** – serotonin, TXA₂.\n3. **Coagulation** – phospholipid surface for tenase and prothrombinase; supply factor V and fibrinogen.\n4. **Clot retraction** – thrombosthenin squeezes out serum within 30–60 min.\n5. **Repair** – PDGF stimulates smooth muscle and fibroblasts.\n\n## Endothelial control\nHealthy endothelium releases **prostacyclin (PGI₂)** and **NO**, which inhibit platelets. Low-dose aspirin irreversibly blocks platelet COX-1 → ↓TXA₂ for the platelet's life, while endothelium regenerates COX.\n\n## Variations\n- **Thrombocytosis** (> 4.5 lakh): after splenectomy, haemorrhage, iron deficiency, inflammation, essential thrombocythaemia.\n- **Thrombocytopenia** (< 1.5 lakh): ↓production (aplastic anaemia, leukaemia, B12/folate deficiency, drugs), ↑destruction (ITP, DIC, dengue, heparin-induced), sequestration (hypersplenism). Spontaneous bleeding usually < 20,000–30,000/µL: petechiae, purpura, mucosal bleeding; ↑bleeding time with normal clotting time.\n\n## Function disorders and drugs\nBernard–Soulier (GP Ib, giant platelets); Glanzmann thrombasthenia (GP IIb/IIIa; no clot retraction); von Willebrand disease. Drugs: aspirin (COX-1), clopidogrel (P2Y₁₂), abciximab/tirofiban (GP IIb/IIIa).\n\n## Applied\nPlatelet concentrates stored at 20–24 °C with agitation for 5 days.","q":{"b":["2–4 µm non-nucleated fragments of megakaryocytes.","Count 1.5–4 lakh/µL; life 8–12 days; 1/3 in spleen.","Thrombopoietin (liver) regulates production.","Adhesion: GP Ib–vWF; aggregation: GP IIb/IIIa–fibrinogen.","α-granules: fibrinogen, vWF, PDGF; dense: ADP, Ca²⁺, serotonin.","Aggregating agents: ADP, TXA₂, thrombin.","Thrombosthenin → clot retraction.","PGI₂ and NO from endothelium inhibit platelets.","Aspirin irreversibly blocks COX-1.","Bernard–Soulier = GP Ib; Glanzmann = GP IIb/IIIa.","Bleeding < 20,000–30,000/µL; BT ↑, CT normal."],"n":["1.5–4 lakh/µL","Life 8–12 days","2–4 µm","Storage 20–24 °C, 5 days"],"m":"Plug **\"AAA\"** – Adhesion, Activation, Aggregation.","v":["Hormone for thrombopoiesis? – thrombopoietin.","Deficient receptor in Glanzmann? – GP IIb/IIIa.","Why aspirin acts 7–10 days? – platelets lack nucleus, cannot remake COX."]}},"bl|Haemostasis & coagulation":{"d":"## Haemostasis\nArrest of bleeding: (1) **vascular spasm** (myogenic, nervous, TXA₂, serotonin, endothelin); (2) **platelet plug** (primary haemostasis); (3) **coagulation** – fibrin mesh (secondary haemostasis); (4) clot retraction, fibrinolysis and repair.\n\n## Clotting factors\nI fibrinogen; II prothrombin; III tissue factor; IV Ca²⁺; V proaccelerin (labile); VII proconvertin (stable); VIII antihaemophilic factor; IX Christmas factor; X Stuart–Prower; XI PTA; XII Hageman; XIII fibrin-stabilising factor. No factor VI. Also prekallikrein and HMW kininogen.\n- Most made in **liver**; **vitamin K-dependent**: **II, VII, IX, X**, proteins C and S (γ-carboxylation lets them bind Ca²⁺). Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase.\n- Factor VIII from endothelium, carried by vWF.\n\n## Cascade\n**Extrinsic** (seconds): tissue factor + VIIa → activates X (and IX). Tested by **PT** (11–16 s; INR).\n**Intrinsic** (minutes): XII (contact with collagen/glass) → XI → IX; IXa + VIIIa + Ca²⁺ + phospholipid (**tenase**) → X. Tested by **aPTT** (25–40 s).\n**Common**: Xa + Va + Ca²⁺ + phospholipid (**prothrombinase**) → thrombin; thrombin → fibrin monomers → polymer → cross-linked by **XIIIa**.\n\n## Cell-based model\nInitiation on TF-bearing cells (small thrombin) → amplification (thrombin activates platelets, V, VIII, XI) → propagation on platelet surface (thrombin burst). Explains why VIII/IX deficiency bleeds but XII deficiency does not.\n\n## Thrombin actions\nFibrinogen → fibrin; activates V, VIII, XI, XIII and platelets; with thrombomodulin activates protein C (negative feedback).\n\n## Natural anticoagulants\nSmooth endothelium, PGI₂, NO; **antithrombin III** (inactivates thrombin, Xa, IXa, XIa; accelerated ≈ 1000× by **heparin**); **protein C + S** (inactivate Va, VIIIa); **TFPI**; fibrin adsorbs thrombin.\n\n## Fibrinolysis\nPlasminogen → **plasmin** via tPA (endothelium) or urokinase; plasmin lyses fibrin → FDPs, D-dimer. Inhibited by α₂-antiplasmin and PAI-1. Drugs: streptokinase, alteplase; tranexamic acid opposes fibrinolysis.\n\n## Anticoagulants\nIn vitro: EDTA, oxalate, citrate (bind Ca²⁺), heparin. In vivo: heparin (aPTT; antidote protamine), warfarin (INR; vitamin K, FFP), DOACs (dabigatran, rivaroxaban).\n\n## Tests\nBleeding time 1–5 min (Duke); clotting time 4–10 min; PT; aPTT; thrombin time; platelet count.\n\n## Disorders\n- **Haemophilia A** (VIII), **B** (IX) – X-linked; haemarthrosis; ↑aPTT and CT, normal PT and BT.\n- von Willebrand disease – ↑BT ± aPTT.\n- Vitamin K deficiency – newborn (prevented by IM vitamin K), obstructive jaundice, antibiotics; ↑PT.\n- DIC – consumption of platelets and factors, ↑D-dimer.\n- Thrombophilia – factor V Leiden, protein C/S/antithrombin deficiency. Virchow's triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability.","q":{"b":["Steps: vascular spasm → platelet plug → coagulation → retraction/fibrinolysis.","No factor VI; IV = Ca²⁺; III = tissue factor.","Vitamin K-dependent: II, VII, IX, X, proteins C, S.","Extrinsic: TF + VIIa; tested by PT/INR.","Intrinsic: XII → XI → IX (+VIII); tested by aPTT.","Common: Xa + Va → thrombin → fibrin; XIII cross-links.","Thrombin activates V, VIII, XI, XIII, platelets, protein C.","Antithrombin III (heparin-enhanced), proteins C & S, TFPI.","Plasmin (from tPA) lyses fibrin → D-dimer.","Haemophilia A = VIII, B = IX; ↑aPTT, normal PT/BT.","Heparin – aPTT/protamine; warfarin – INR/vitamin K.","EDTA, citrate, oxalate bind Ca²⁺."],"n":["BT 1–5 min; CT 4–10 min","PT 11–16 s; aPTT 25–40 s","Fibrinogen 200–400 mg/dL"],"m":"Vitamin K factors **\"1972\"** = 10, 9, 7, 2; **PeT** = extrinsic, **PiTT** = intrinsic.","v":["Vitamin K-dependent factors? – II, VII, IX, X.","Test for extrinsic pathway? – PT.","How does heparin act? – potentiates antithrombin III."]}},"bl|Blood groups":{"d":"## Basis\nBlood groups are inherited antigens on the RBC membrane; > 40 systems exist, but ABO and Rh matter most. Landsteiner described ABO (1900–01); Landsteiner and Wiener described Rh (1940).\n\n## ABO system\n- A and B antigens are sugars added to the **H antigen** by glycosyltransferases (A adds N-acetylgalactosamine, B adds galactose; O gene is inactive).\n- Present in saliva of **secretors** (≈ 80%).\n- **Landsteiner's law**: if an antigen is on the RBC, its antibody is absent from plasma, and vice versa.\n- Natural antibodies anti-A and anti-B are **IgM**, appear from 2–8 months (gut flora/food antigens), do not cross placenta.\n- Group A: A antigen, anti-B. Group B: B antigen, anti-A. Group AB: both antigens, no antibody (universal recipient). Group O: H only, anti-A + anti-B (universal donor).\n- Subgroups A₁ (≈ 80%) and A₂ (≈ 20%).\n- **Bombay (Oh)**: no H antigen (hh); has anti-A, anti-B, anti-H; can receive only Bombay blood.\n- A and B codominant, O recessive – useful for paternity exclusion.\n- India (approx.): B 32–35%, O 30–37%, A 21–23%, AB 7–9%.\n\n## Rh system\nAntigens C, c, D, E, e; **D** most immunogenic. Rh-positive ≈ 93–95% in India. **No natural anti-D** – formed only after exposure (transfusion or pregnancy); anti-D is **IgG** and crosses the placenta.\n\n## Haemolytic disease of newborn\nRh-negative mother, Rh-positive fetus. Fetal cells enter mother at delivery, abortion, amniocentesis → anti-D formed; first baby usually spared. Next Rh+ fetus: IgG anti-D crosses placenta → haemolysis → anaemia, erythroblastaemia, jaundice, **kernicterus**, hydrops fetalis.\n- **Prevention**: anti-D immunoglobulin to Rh-negative mother within **72 h** of delivery (also at 28 weeks and after abortion).\n- **Treatment**: exchange transfusion with O Rh-negative blood, phototherapy, intrauterine transfusion.\n- ABO HDN (mother O, baby A/B) is commoner but milder; can affect the first baby.\n\n## Other systems\nKell, Duffy (Duffy-negative cells resist P. vivax), Kidd, MNS, Lewis, P, Lutheran – important in multiply transfused patients.\n\n## Grouping and cross-matching\n- Forward grouping: patient cells + anti-A, anti-B, anti-D; agglutination = antigen present.\n- Reverse grouping: patient serum + known A and B cells.\n- **Major cross-match**: donor cells + recipient serum; minor: recipient cells + donor plasma.\n- Coombs test: direct (antibody on RBC – HDN, autoimmune haemolysis); indirect (antibody in serum – antenatal screening).\n\n## Applied\nMismatched transfusion → acute haemolytic reaction; forensic and paternity use.","q":{"b":["ABO by Landsteiner (1900); Rh by Landsteiner & Wiener (1940).","A and B antigens built on H antigen.","Landsteiner's law: antigen present ⇒ antibody absent.","Anti-A/anti-B: natural IgM.","O universal donor; AB universal recipient.","Bombay: no H antigen, has anti-H.","A and B codominant; O recessive.","No natural anti-D; anti-D is IgG.","HDN: Rh– mother, Rh+ fetus, usually second pregnancy.","Anti-D Ig within 72 h of delivery.","Major cross-match: donor cells + recipient serum.","Duffy-negative → resistance to P. vivax."],"n":["Rh+ ≈ 93–95% Indians","India: B ≈ 32–35%, O ≈ 30–37%","Anti-D within 72 h","Secretors ≈ 80%"],"m":"**\"ABO = IgM = Made naturally; Rh = IgG = Gained and Goes across placenta\"**.","v":["Why is first Rh+ baby usually spared? – sensitisation mostly happens at first delivery.","Bombay group? – hh, no H antigen, anti-H present.","Class of natural anti-A? – IgM."]}},"bl|Plasma proteins":{"d":"## Normal values\nTotal 6.4–8.3 g/dL: albumin 3.5–5.0, globulins 2.0–3.5, fibrinogen 0.2–0.4 g/dL. A:G ratio 1.2–2:1.\n\n## Separation\n- Salting out with ammonium sulphate.\n- **Electrophoresis** (pH 8.6) – from fastest: albumin, α₁, α₂, β, γ.\n- Ultracentrifugation, immunoelectrophoresis, chromatography.\n\n## Origin\nAlbumin, fibrinogen, most α and β globulins and clotting factors – **liver**. γ-globulins – **plasma cells**.\n\n## Important proteins\n- **Albumin** (≈ 66–69 kDa; t½ ≈ 20 days) – 70–80% of oncotic pressure; carries bilirubin, fatty acids, Ca²⁺, drugs, hormones.\n- **α₁**: α₁-antitrypsin (deficiency → emphysema, liver disease), thyroxine-binding globulin, transcortin, α-fetoprotein.\n- **α₂**: haptoglobin (binds free Hb, ↓ in haemolysis), ceruloplasmin (copper; ↓ in Wilson disease), α₂-macroglobulin, angiotensinogen.\n- **β**: transferrin, hemopexin, LDL, complement C3.\n- **γ**: immunoglobulins.\n- **Fibrinogen** (≈ 340 kDa) – clotting; main cause of ↑ESR.\n\n## Functions\n1. **Oncotic pressure** ≈ 25–28 mmHg – keeps fluid in vessels (Starling forces).\n2. **Coagulation** and anticoagulation, fibrinolysis.\n3. **Defence** – antibodies, complement.\n4. **Transport** – hormones, vitamins, metals, lipids, drugs.\n5. **Buffering** – ≈ 15% of blood buffering.\n6. **Viscosity** and peripheral resistance.\n7. **ESR** – fibrinogen and globulins promote rouleaux.\n8. **Protein reserve** in starvation.\n\n## Acute-phase proteins\nRise with IL-6 in inflammation: CRP, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, ferritin, serum amyloid A, α₁-antitrypsin. Fall (negative): albumin, transferrin, prealbumin.\n\n## Variations\n- **Hypoalbuminaemia**: ↓synthesis (cirrhosis, malnutrition – kwashiorkor), loss (nephrotic syndrome, burns, protein-losing enteropathy) → oedema, ascites.\n- **Hyperproteinaemia**: dehydration, multiple myeloma (M-spike), chronic infection, kala-azar.\n- **Reversed A:G ratio**: cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, myeloma, chronic infection.\n- Hypofibrinogenaemia: DIC, liver failure.\n\n## Applied\nAlbumin infusion in burns and hypoalbuminaemia; serum electrophoresis for myeloma; α-fetoprotein for liver cancer and neural-tube defects.","q":{"b":["Total 6.4–8.3 g/dL; albumin 3.5–5; globulin 2–3.5; fibrinogen 0.2–0.4.","A:G ratio 1.2–2:1; reversed in cirrhosis, nephrosis, myeloma.","Electrophoresis: albumin, α₁, α₂, β, γ.","Liver makes most; plasma cells make γ-globulins.","Albumin: 70–80% of oncotic pressure; main carrier.","Fibrinogen: clotting and ESR.","Haptoglobin ↓ in haemolysis; ceruloplasmin ↓ in Wilson.","α₁-antitrypsin deficiency → emphysema.","Acute-phase: CRP, fibrinogen, ferritin; negative: albumin, transferrin.","Hypoalbuminaemia → oedema.","Myeloma → M-spike in γ region."],"n":["Total ≈ 7 g/dL","Albumin ≈ 69 kDa, t½ ≈ 20 d","Oncotic pressure 25–28 mmHg","Fibrinogen 200–400 mg/dL"],"m":"Electrophoresis **\"All Animals Are Big Grazers\"** – Albumin, α₁, α₂, β, γ.","v":["Main protein for oncotic pressure? – albumin.","Protein absent in serum? – fibrinogen.","A negative acute-phase protein? – albumin."]}},"bl|Haemoglobin: structure, variants & breakdown":{"d":"## Structure\nHaemoglobin (MW ≈ 64,500) is a tetramer of four globin chains, each with one **haem**; one molecule binds four O₂.\n- Haem = protoporphyrin IX + **ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)**; iron bonds to four pyrrole nitrogens, the proximal histidine and reversibly to O₂ (oxygenation, not oxidation).\n- Adult HbA = α₂β₂ (α 141, β 146 amino acids).\n- 1 g Hb binds **1.34 mL O₂**; with Hb 15 g/dL, O₂ capacity ≈ 20 mL/dL.\n- T (deoxy) and R (oxy) states → cooperative binding and sigmoid dissociation curve; 2,3-BPG binds deoxy-Hb β chains.\n\n## Normal haemoglobins\n- **HbA** (α₂β₂) ≈ 97%; **HbA₂** (α₂δ₂) 1.5–3.5% (↑ in β-thalassaemia trait); **HbF** (α₂γ₂) < 1% in adults, 70–80% at birth, replaced by ≈ 6–12 months.\n- HbF has **higher O₂ affinity** because γ chains bind 2,3-BPG poorly – helps placental O₂ transfer.\n- Embryonic: Gower 1 (ζ₂ε₂), Gower 2 (α₂ε₂), Portland (ζ₂γ₂).\n- HbA₁c – glycated Hb (< 5.7% normal), reflects glucose over 2–3 months.\n\n## Synthesis\nHaem: succinyl-CoA + glycine → ALA (**ALA synthase**, rate-limiting, needs vitamin B6) → porphobilinogen (ALA dehydratase – inhibited by lead) → … → protoporphyrin IX + Fe²⁺ (ferrochelatase – lead-sensitive) → haem. Haem inhibits ALA synthase (feedback). Globin: α genes on chromosome 16; β, γ, δ on chromosome 11.\n\n## Derivatives\n- **Oxy-Hb** (bright red); **deoxy-Hb** (purple; > 5 g/dL in capillaries → cyanosis).\n- **Carbamino-Hb** – CO₂ on globin amino groups (20–30% of CO₂ transport).\n- **Carboxy-Hb** – CO affinity ≈ 200–250× O₂; cherry-red; left shift; treat with 100% or hyperbaric O₂.\n- **Met-Hb** – Fe³⁺, cannot carry O₂ (< 1–2% normal); nitrites, dapsone, local anaesthetics; chocolate-brown blood; treat with methylene blue.\n- Sulph-Hb – irreversible.\n\n## Haemoglobinopathies\n- **HbS** – β6 glutamic acid → valine; deoxy-HbS polymerises → sickling, haemolysis, vaso-occlusion. Trait protects against falciparum malaria; common in tribal central India.\n- HbC (β6 glu → lys); HbE (β26 glu → lys; NE India).\n- **Thalassaemias** – reduced chain synthesis: α (deletions; HbH = β₄, Hb Bart's = γ₄ → hydrops) and β (major – transfusion-dependent, ↑HbF; trait – ↑HbA₂, microcytosis).\n\n## Breakdown\nOld RBCs (≈ 120 days) are phagocytosed by macrophages. Globin → amino acids. Haem oxygenase → **biliverdin** + Fe (recycled/stored) + **CO** (the only endogenous CO). Biliverdin reductase → unconjugated bilirubin → albumin → liver conjugation → bile → urobilinogen → stercobilin/urobilin. ≈ 6–7 g Hb/day → 250–300 mg bilirubin. Free plasma Hb is bound by **haptoglobin**, haem by hemopexin; excess → haemoglobinuria.\n\n## Normal values\nMen 13–17, women 12–15, newborn 16–22 g/dL; physiological fall to 10–11 g/dL at 2–3 months.","q":{"b":["Hb: 4 globins + 4 haem; binds 4 O₂; MW ≈ 64,500.","Haem = protoporphyrin IX + Fe²⁺ (oxygenation, not oxidation).","1 g Hb carries 1.34 mL O₂.","HbA 97%, HbA₂ 1.5–3.5%, HbF < 1% adult.","HbF: higher O₂ affinity (weak 2,3-BPG binding).","ALA synthase (B6) is rate-limiting; lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase.","CO binds 200–250× more avidly than O₂.","MetHb = Fe³⁺; methylene blue treats.","HbS: β6 glu → val; trait protects against malaria.","β-thal trait ↑HbA₂; major ↑HbF.","Haem → biliverdin + Fe + CO → bilirubin.","Haptoglobin binds free Hb."],"n":["Hb M 13–17, F 12–15, newborn 16–22 g/dL","1.34 mL O₂/g","HbA₂ 1.5–3.5%","Cyanosis > 5 g/dL deoxy-Hb"],"m":"**\"Fetal Grabs Oxygen\"** (HbF high affinity); sickle **\"Glu→Val, Very Bad\"**.","v":["State of iron in Hb? – ferrous.","Mutation in HbS? – β6 glutamate → valine.","Why HbF has higher affinity? – poor 2,3-BPG binding."]}},"bl|Immunity":{"d":"## Overview\nImmunity is resistance to infection. **Innate** (natural, non-specific) and **acquired** (adaptive, specific) arms cooperate.\n\n## Innate immunity\nPresent from birth, immediate, no memory.\n- Barriers: skin, mucus and cilia, lysozyme, gastric acid, normal flora.\n- Cells: neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells (kill cells lacking MHC-I), dendritic cells, mast cells, eosinophils.\n- Toll-like receptors recognise microbial patterns (LPS).\n- Soluble: complement (alternative, lectin), interferons α/β, CRP, cytokines (IL-1, TNF-α, IL-6); inflammation and fever.\n\n## Acquired immunity\nSpecific, has memory, distinguishes self from non-self.\n- **T cells** mature in **thymus** (positive and negative selection); **B cells** in **bone marrow**. Secondary organs: lymph nodes, spleen, MALT.\n- **Humoral** (B cells): antigen + helper T signals → plasma cells → antibodies; memory B cells. Against extracellular bacteria, toxins.\n- **Cell-mediated** (T cells): antigen must be presented with **MHC**.\n- **CD4 helper** cells see MHC II: Th1 (IFN-γ – macrophage activation, intracellular microbes), Th2 (IL-4, IL-5 – B cells, IgE, eosinophils), Th17 (IL-17 – neutrophils).\n- **CD8 cytotoxic** cells see MHC I: kill infected, tumour and graft cells (perforin, granzymes, Fas-L).\n- Regulatory T cells (FOXP3) maintain tolerance.\n\n## MHC (HLA, chromosome 6)\nClass I (HLA-A, B, C) on all nucleated cells – endogenous peptides to CD8. Class II (DR, DP, DQ) on APCs – exogenous peptides to CD4. Key in transplants; HLA-B27 – ankylosing spondylitis.\n\n## Types of acquired immunity\n- Active natural – infection. Active artificial – vaccines (live: BCG, MMR, OPV; killed: IPV; toxoid: tetanus; subunit: hepatitis B; mRNA).\n- Passive natural – maternal IgG, colostral IgA. Passive artificial – anti-tetanus, anti-rabies Ig, anti-snake venom, anti-D (immediate, short-lived).\n\n## Complement\nClassical (antibody), alternative and lectin pathways → C3 convertase → C3b (opsonin), C3a/C5a (anaphylatoxins, chemotaxis), C5b–9 membrane attack complex.\n\n## Hypersensitivity\nI – IgE, mast cells (anaphylaxis, asthma). II – cytotoxic antibody (transfusion reaction, HDN). III – immune complex (serum sickness, SLE, post-streptococcal GN). IV – delayed T-cell (Mantoux, contact dermatitis, graft rejection).\n\n## Applied\nDiGeorge (thymic aplasia), SCID, Bruton's; HIV destroys CD4 cells (AIDS < 200/µL); autoimmunity (type 1 diabetes, Graves', myasthenia).","q":{"b":["Innate: immediate, non-specific, no memory.","Adaptive: specific, memory, self–non-self discrimination.","T cells mature in thymus; B cells in marrow.","Humoral (antibodies) vs cell-mediated (T cells).","CD4 + MHC II; CD8 + MHC I.","MHC I on all nucleated cells; MHC II on APCs.","Th1 – IFN-γ; Th2 – IL-4/5, IgE.","NK cells kill cells lacking MHC I.","Active vs passive; natural vs artificial.","Complement: C3b opsonin, C5a chemotaxis, MAC lysis.","Hypersensitivity I–IV (IgE, cytotoxic, immune complex, delayed).","AIDS when CD4 < 200/µL."],"n":["CD4 500–1500/µL","HLA on chromosome 6","Maternal IgG protects ≈ 6 months"],"m":"**\"ACID\"** hypersensitivity – Anaphylactic, Cytotoxic, Immune complex, Delayed; MHC **II×4 = I×8 = 8**.","v":["Where do T cells mature? – thymus.","MHC presenting to CD8? – class I.","Passive artificial example? – anti-tetanus immunoglobulin."]}},"bl|Blood transfusion & blood bank":{"d":"## Indications\nAcute blood loss (trauma, surgery, obstetric haemorrhage); severe anaemia (commonly Hb < 7 g/dL, or < 8 with heart disease); thalassaemia major, sickle-cell crisis, aplastic anaemia; platelet and clotting disorders (components); exchange transfusion in HDN; burns (plasma).\n\n## Donor selection (Indian norms)\nAge 18–65; weight ≥ 45 kg (350 mL) or ≥ 55 kg (450 mL); Hb ≥ 12.5 g/dL; normal pulse, BP, temperature; interval ≥ 90 days (men), ≥ 120 days (women). Defer: pregnancy/lactation, recent illness, tattoo within 12 months, malaria, jaundice, high-risk behaviour. Mandatory tests: HIV 1/2, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis, malaria; ABO/Rh grouping; antibody screen.\n\n## Collection and storage\n- **CPDA-1**: citrate (chelates Ca²⁺), phosphate (buffer), dextrose (glycolysis), adenine (ATP) – shelf life **35 days**; ACD 21 days; **SAGM** red cells 42 days.\n- Red cells/whole blood at **2–6 °C**.\n- **Storage lesion**: ↓2,3-BPG (↑O₂ affinity), ↓ATP, K⁺ leaks (↑plasma K⁺), ↓pH, loss of platelets and labile factors V and VIII, microaggregates.\n\n## Components\n- **Packed red cells** – 1 unit raises Hb ≈ 1 g/dL in adults.\n- **Platelet concentrate** – 20–24 °C with agitation, 5 days.\n- **Fresh frozen plasma** – frozen within 6–8 h at ≤ −30 °C (1 year); all factors – DIC, liver disease, warfarin reversal.\n- **Cryoprecipitate** – VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, XIII – haemophilia A, hypofibrinogenaemia.\n- Albumin, IVIG, factor concentrates; leucoreduced and irradiated products (prevent febrile reactions and GVHD).\n\n## Pre-transfusion testing\nABO and Rh of donor and recipient; antibody screen; **major cross-match** (donor cells + recipient serum; saline and indirect Coombs). Extreme emergency: O Rh-negative red cells.\n\n## Transfusion reactions\n1. **Acute haemolytic** (ABO mismatch, usually clerical error): recipient IgM anti-A/B + complement lyse donor cells → fever, chills, loin and chest pain, hypotension, shock, haemoglobinuria, DIC, **acute kidney injury**. Stop transfusion, support circulation and urine output.\n2. Delayed haemolytic (3–14 days; Kidd, Rh antibodies).\n3. **Febrile non-haemolytic** – commonest; leucocyte antibodies/cytokines.\n4. Allergic/urticarial; anaphylaxis in IgA-deficient recipients.\n5. TRALI; TACO (overload).\n6. **Massive transfusion** (≥ one blood volume in 24 h): hypocalcaemia (citrate), hyperkalaemia, hypothermia, dilutional coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia, acid–base changes.\n7. Infections, iron overload, GVHD, alloimmunisation.\n\n## Autologous transfusion\nPre-deposit, acute normovolaemic haemodilution, intraoperative cell salvage.\n\n## Applied\nPlasma expanders: crystalloids, dextran, gelatin, starch. Voluntary non-remunerated donation is safest; one unit separated into components can help up to three patients.","q":{"b":["Donor 18–65 yr, ≥ 45 kg, Hb ≥ 12.5 g/dL; interval 3 months men, 4 months women.","Screen HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, malaria.","CPDA-1 35 days; SAGM 42 days; ACD 21 days.","Red cells 2–6 °C; platelets 20–24 °C (5 days); FFP ≤ −30 °C.","Storage lesion: ↓2,3-BPG, ↑K⁺, ↓pH.","1 unit PRBC ≈ +1 g/dL Hb.","Cryo: VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, XIII.","Major cross-match: donor cells + recipient serum.","ABO mismatch: haemoglobinuria, shock, AKI, DIC.","Commonest reaction: febrile non-haemolytic.","Massive transfusion: hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, hypothermia.","Anaphylaxis in IgA deficiency."],"n":["CPDA-1 35 d; SAGM 42 d","Platelets 5 d at 20–24 °C","Red cells 2–6 °C","Unit 350/450 mL"],"m":"**CPDA** – Citrate stops clotting, Phosphate buffers, Dextrose fuels, Adenine makes ATP.","v":["Platelet storage temperature? – 20–24 °C with agitation.","Commonest transfusion reaction? – febrile non-haemolytic.","Why hypocalcaemia in massive transfusion? – citrate binds Ca²⁺."]}}});